Title = {Precision or division? Stakeholder perceptions and tensions in the
   digital transition of Swedish forests},
Journal = {FOREST POLICY AND ECONOMICS},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {190},
Month = {SEP},
Abstract = {Precision forestry technologies are promoted as solutions for managing
   competing forest values and mitigating land-use conflicts, yet their
   social and political implications remain poorly understood. This study
   examines how Swedish forestry stakeholders perceive the potential and
   risks of digital tools such as AI, remote sensing, and predictive
   modeling in relation to forest-related conflicts. Drawing on a
   future-oriented participatory workshop and an affordance-actualization
   perspective, we analyze stakeholder expectation of what precision
   forestry is expected to enable and the conditions under which these
   possibilities might become actionable. Three main perceived affordances
   are identified: (1) improving landowners' ability to use forests through
   new forms of measurement and compensation, (2) providing data for
   decision-making and forecasting trade-offs, and (3) creating common
   discussion grounds for dialogue and negotiation. Across these
   affordances, there are several tensions between quantifiable and
   non-quantifiable knowledge and values, large and small actors, and
   transparency and control over data that are discussed in relation to a
   wider literature on digitalization and environmental governance. Data
   quality, standardization, regulatory uncertainty, and trust in models
   are barriers perceived by stakeholders. Our findings indicate that the
   digital transition in forestry is unlikely to reduce forest conflicts on
   its own and may, under certain conditions, contribute to the
   reinforcement of existing power asymmetries and exclusion of actors if
   the identified tensions are not addressed.},
