Abstract = {Through human contact with nature -whether in its least altered state,
   as transformed through the construction of landscapes, or adapted to
   human ideas and needs in the proximity of orchards and gardens- its
   beneficial effects on both physical and mental health have been
   recognized since Antiquity. However, it was not until the first half of
   the nineteenth century that empirical evidence linked public health to
   the hygienic and living conditions of cities and buildings, and urban
   and architectural actions began to move in this direction. The goal of
   creating urban and architectural spaces for well-being represents a
   significant step beyond the objectives of healthiness or comfort, since
   by placing users at the center of the project, their individual needs
   take precedence. The application of these universal design strategies to
   gardens associated with healthcare facilities has required a review of
   background references, current guidelines, and built examples; and they
   have been the focus of two competitive research projects, whose results
   are a hospital information system and a Good Practices Guide of general
   application, both for hospital gardens and for urban public gardens.},
